# HopHacks ink pipeline

Draw on an Android phone over the LAN; the laptop stores each drawing and runs
analysis on it. Partly an accessibility tool for stroke patients with tremor and
limited finger mobility. `readME.md` explains how to run it;
`technical_report.md` is the architecture. This file is the state and the
working agreements.

## Working agreements

- **Confirm before building anything new.** Explain the plan and wait for a go.
- **Reminder to push** goes at the end of any list of commands, nowhere else.
- Always invoke Python as `.venv/bin/python` on Linux or
  `.\.venv\Scripts\python.exe` on Windows. A bare `python` grabs the system
  interpreter, which lacks the dependencies and has already caused a second
  server to seize port 8000 while the venv one bound to nothing.
- Verify with the scripts in `scripts/` before claiming something works.

## Layout

| Path | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| `technical_report.md` | Architecture, control flow, templates, features |
| `run.py` | Launcher: LAN address, QR codes for tablet and caretaker |
| `server/config.py` | Settings from env and `.env`, smoothing presets |
| `server/storage.py` | Disk writes, `index.jsonl`, `answers.jsonl`, DB seam |
| `server/app.py` | Routes, WebSocket hub, `ask_tablet()`, `analyse()` |
| `server/pipeline.py` | Step 2 hook: `IntentRecognizer`, falls back to `SAMPLE_TEXT` |
| `server/speech.py` | ElevenLabs synthesis, `data/tts/` cache, never raises |
| `stroke_geometry.py` | LLM-free 1 / 2 detection and circle/square/triangle grading |
| `local_vision.py` | Ollama-backed reading of the PNG when the cloud vendors fail |
| `xai_backend.py` | Grok over plain HTTP, between the Gemini chain and the local model |
| `web/canvas.html/js` | Tablet surface: drawing, idle capture, taps, speech |
| `web/viewer.html/js` | Optional desktop view, purely a listener |
| `web/caretaker.html/js` | Caregiver web fallback |
| `web/brain.html/js` | Memory graph; embed mode for the Android app |
| `android/` | Native caretaker app: Alerts + Brain, emergency notifications |
| `data/priors/` | Labelled repeated drawings used as feature templates |

Tests, each starting its own server on a free port: `browser_test.py` (capture
loop), `tap_test.py` (tap answers, multi-touch), `smoothing_test.py` (tremor
filter), `pipeline_test.py` (what step 2 receives), `speech_test.py` (synthesis,
caching, keyless fallback, confirming tap), `caretaker_test.py` (caregiver phone
gets image + prompts + yes/no), `emergency_test.py` (help alerts the caretaker
without dialing the pad), `recognize_test.py` (Gemini ranking
harness, near-tie blend, timeout fallback), `priors_test.py` (hold-one-out of
`data/priors/`), `digit_test.py` (geometric 1 / 2 detection and
offline shape grading, stdlib only), `model_chain_test.py` (model fallback,
stubbed client), `local_vision_test.py` (on-machine fallback and its ordering
against templates, stubbed HTTP so Ollama need not be running),
`xai_chain_test.py` (the Grok tier, both vendors stubbed so it spends no credits),
`eval_benchmark.py` (labeled drawings,
ranking report for judges). Also `smoke_test.py`, `ask.py`,
`show_data.py`, `show_strokes.py` against a running server, and
`seed_drawings.py` to fill `drawings_db.json` from `data/priors/`.
`scripts/build_caretaker_apk.py` builds the debug APK.

## Status

**Step 1 is complete and tested.** Capture after 5s of inactivity, PNG plus
stroke JSON plus `index.jsonl`, optional viewer, tap-to-answer (one tap yes, two
no), stroke start/end tones and vibration, One Euro tremor smoothing.

**Step 2 recognition is wired.** `pipeline.process_capture` ranks via
Gemini from the PNG (history is a weak prior; `yes`/`no` are not drawing
tags). When Gemini fails or exceeds `GEMINI_TIMEOUT_S` the PNG is read by a
local vision model, and templates in `drawings_db.json` answer only if that is
unavailable too. A close prior can break a Gemini near-tie among the tags it
already proposed; it cannot invent a new one. The spoken line may name the object
(apple, pizza). A no tap
retries the next candidate (up to three). Follow-ups
stay on the confirmed intent and are skipped once the request is specific
(water does not ask tea; rest has none). Help offers to call the caretaker
from `patient_data.json`; a yes alerts the caretaker app instead of opening
`tel:` on the tablet. After a yes the
pad speaks a caregiver closing and does not wait on the LLM graph patch. `SAMPLE_TEXT` remains the last fallback when ranking
is empty, throws, or `INK_RECOGNITION=0`. `/brain` listens on the viewer
websocket for `graph` updates and no longer auto-seeds demo data on GET.

**The 1 / 2 that starts the drawing game is read geometrically, not by
Gemini.** `stroke_geometry.detect_digit` measures the strokes: a 1 is one tall
upright near-straight stroke, a 2 is one open curve from the top down to a
left-to-right baseline. Above `FAST_PATH_CONFIDENCE` the Gemini call is skipped
altogether, so a clean 1 starts the game instantly and with no key. Below it
Gemini still ranks, and geometry either fills in a digit Gemini left empty
(the common miss, since the digit is one field in an intent prompt) or vetoes
one it claimed. A veto only fires on things a character cannot be: a closed
loop, or more than three substantial strokes. `RecognitionResult.digit_source`
records which of the two decided, and `digit_reason` says why.

Grading each attempt also falls back to geometry, on corner count and
circularity. Before that the game could be started but never won without
Gemini: every attempt came back a miss.

**Every Gemini call walks a model chain**, `gemini-3.5-flash-lite` then
`gemini-3.6-flash`, set by `GEMINI_MODELS` or pinned with `GEMINI_MODEL`. One
`GEMINI_TIMEOUT_S` budget covers the whole chain. A rejected key breaks out
rather than being retried on every model. Verified live: the chain falls through
a 404 and a 429 to a working model.

The lite model leads on availability. Measured against the three labelled
drawings in `eval/`: lite 3/3 intents, `gemini-3.6-flash` 1/3 (503s),
`gemini-3.8-flash` 0/3 (daily quota already spent). Lite is vaguer on the
specific object -- it called a pizza wedge a bowl of soup -- so `detail` is
weaker than `tag`, which the follow-up questions absorb.

**xAI's Grok sits between Gemini and the local model**, in `xai_backend.py`. It
hooks into `_generate`, so it covers every call rather than ranking alone, and it
is the only fallback strong enough for the real prompt with its patient context.
A missing or malformed `GEMINI_API_KEY` is no longer fatal while `XAI_API_KEY` is
set; the complaint is held back and only raised if xAI cannot answer either.

Model choice there is latency, not accuracy. On the `eval/` drawings with the real
ranking prompt: `grok-4.6` 3/3 in 42-86s, `grok-4.20-0309-non-reasoning` 2/3 in
2.3-2.6s, `grok-4.3` 0/3 in 13-18s. The fast one serves captures because
`recognize` caps the whole call at `GEMINI_TIMEOUT_S`; `grok-4.6` is reserved for
`_generate(unhurried=True)`, which `compress_history` and `update_graphs_with_llm`
pass because they run after the pad has already spoken. `grok-4.6` is the only
model tried, Gemini included, that reads the pizza drawing as food.

Only a **fast** Gemini failure leaves room for xAI, which is the common one: a
429, 404 or 400 refuses in well under a second. A Gemini call that hangs instead
burns the budget, `recognize`'s executor times out, and the local model takes it.

`RecognitionResult` candidates carry `source`, now one of `gemini`, `xai`, `local`
or `feature_fallback`, fed by `rank_drawing_tags`'s `vendor` key off
`GeminiPatientModel.last_vendor`. Without it a Grok answer was filed as Gemini.

**Below all of that sits a vision model on this machine**, `local_vision.py` talking
to Ollama, so a spent quota no longer means templates. `gemma3:12b` was chosen by
measurement on the `eval/` drawings: 2/3 at about 2.5s warm, against 1/3 at 5.7s
for `qwen2.5vl:7b`, which read the cup as a bowl. Verified end to end through
`/api/capture` with a deliberately invalid key: Gemini 400s, the local model
answers `water` from "A cup", `fallback_used=True`.

It does **not** reuse the `rank_drawing_tags` prompt. Handed the full 4.5KB of
patient context, qwen answered "I would like an apple, please" for a cup, a
cross, and a pizza alike -- the text drowns out the image at this size. The local
prompt is the tag list and the picture, nothing else, which costs history as a
tie-breaker. Its answers carry `LOCAL_LIKELIHOOD` and `source="local"` on purpose:
it tagged the cross `help` while calling it "a snake", so the pad must keep
asking rather than asserting.

`gemma4` was measured too: also 2/3 but faster at 1.2s, and rejected because it
describes a cup as "a finger drawing" and usually returns an empty `detail`, which
is what names the object and gates the follow-up questions.

`usable_sentence` gates whatever sentence the model writes, because gemma4 echoed
the schema's own `"I would like ..., please."` and the pad was about to say it. A
rejected sentence is no loss: `spoken_for` then uses the curated `PHRASES` line,
which is better written anyway. It can only catch shape, not sense -- qwen's "I
need help finding the flag." passes.

**Templates are seeded by `scripts/seed_drawings.py` from `data/priors/`.** Those
are this person's repeated drawings (apple, pizza, water, call, music, 1, 2).
They fill `drawings_db.json` and rewrite each tag's `drawing_ids`. A strong
match (0.6+) is mixed into Gemini's rank at 30% so a 0.55/0.50 split can be
broken, but only when exactly one candidate is that close. A 0.95 food reading
still beats a cup template, and templates
cannot invent a tag Gemini omitted. Digit priors stay in the store but do not
start the drawing game: an open apple is close enough to a stored 2 that this
used to offer "Play a game?" instead of food. Cartoon outlines remain only
for tags the priors do not cover (`bed`, `check`, `xmark`). `eval/catalog.json`
is a backup when a tag has no prior. Verified with `scripts/priors_test.py`.

**Speech and confirmation are built and tested.** `analyse()` hands the text to
`speech.synthesise()`, pushes `{"type": "speak", "text", "url"}` to tablets, then
`ask_tablet("Did I get that right?")`, whose tap lands in `answers.jsonl` with
the capture id and text in `context`. No ElevenLabs key yet: until
`ELEVENLABS_API_KEY` is in `.env`, the tablet speaks with its own
`speechSynthesis`. Model still undecided — `eleven_flash_v2_5` by default,
switchable to `eleven_multilingual_v2` if hackathon credits come through.

**Caretaker Android app is built.** `android/` is a Kotlin app with three tabs:
Alerts (5 at a time + Load more), Brain (`/brain?embed=1` with pinch/+/- zoom
and Load more for the next 5 events), and Today (daily confirmed request
counts: water, pizza, … resetting each day via `/api/caretaker/stats`). A
foreground service keeps the caretaker WebSocket alive and posts OS
notifications. Help is `kind=emergency` with a separate IMPORTANCE_HIGH
channel, alarm sound, and full-screen HELP activity — not a `tel:` dialer on
the pad. `CLIENT_VERSION` is 10; `CARETAKER_VERSION` is 2. Debug APK:
`android/app/build/outputs/apk/debug/app-debug.apk`. `/caretaker` remains as
a web fallback.

**Tiger Data is parked** until everything else is done. Nothing is wired; only
an unused `INK_DATABASE_URL` and an empty `CaptureStore.sinks`.

**No getter function exists.** Reading data back means `CaptureStore.recent()`,
the HTTP routes, or the scripts. The user intends to specify one.

## Gotchas worth remembering

- Bump `CLIENT_VERSION` in **both** `web/canvas.js` and `server/app.py` on any
  frontend change. A tablet's socket silently reconnects after a server
  restart, so a stale page looks healthy while running old code; the version
  check forces a reload. This has already cost an hour of debugging.
- Same idea for the caregiver web fallback: bump `CARETAKER_VERSION` in
  `web/caretaker.js` and `server/app.py` together. The native app talks to
  `/ws?role=caretaker` and does not use that version check.
- The key `strokes` is a list of stroke dicts inside a capture's `.json`, but a
  filename string inside `index.jsonl`. Same name, different type.
- `gemini-2.5-flash` looks available -- `models.list()` returns it -- and 404s
  on use with "no longer available to new users". Listing a model is not proof a
  key can call it, so check with `gemini_key_test.py`, which pings the chain.
- The free tier allows `gemini-3.8-flash` 20 requests per **day**, per model, and
  the 429 names the limit. Judging a model's accuracy on a day it has been rated
  out reads as the model being wrong when it never answered. Check `fallback_used`
  before believing a bad result, and prefer lite models for repeated testing.
- A Gemini ranking under `MIN_USABLE_LIKELIHOOD` (0.25) is treated as no answer.
  Telephone-shaped drawings were read as digits, then leftover tags arrived at
  0.05 and the pad spoke them as if they were certain. A claimed digit that
  geometry vetoes is discarded the same way: the model was answering the wrong
  question. Replay the captures in `data/captures/` before trusting a prompt
  change here.
- `detail` is a food/water variety, not a caption. After a yes on help it once
  asked "Is that hand?" because the model had described the ink. An H drawing
  set `detail: "h"` and `"h" in "I need help."` skipped the caretaker call,
  then a closing could say "I'll help with the h." `already_named` is a word
  boundary, single letters are not specific, and help spoken that names a
  letter falls back to "I need help, please." Only
  `DETAIL_REFINABLE_TAGS` (`food`, `water`) may turn detail into a follow-up.
- Not every drawing is a need. Mountains, a book, a sun, or a scene is `story`;
  a smile or a face is `talk`. Forcing those into rest/water/help is how a
  landscape became "I would like to lie down." After a yes, `companion_for`
  speaks a short story or a kind remark; `_clean_spoken` must not be used on
  that text or it keeps only the first sentence.
- Gemini refuses a per-request deadline under 10s with a 400, so the model chain
  cannot split a 15s budget into two real attempts. Raise `GEMINI_TIMEOUT_S`
  past 20s if a hanging model needs to be survivable, not just a refusal.
- Ollama's `keep_alive` must be the **number** `-1`, not the string `"-1"`, which
  it parses as a duration and rejects with `time: missing unit in duration "-1"`.
  Its errors live in the response body and never in the status line, so anything
  that does not read the body reports a bare 400.
- Ollama defaults to a 4096-token context and refuses a longer request outright
  rather than truncating it: the production ranking prompt is 4377 tokens with an
  image and 400s. Raising `num_ctx` also grows the KV cache, which on a 12GB card
  competes with the 8GB of weights, so do not raise it for headroom alone.
- A bigger local model is not automatically better here. `gemma3:12b` beat
  `qwen2.5vl:7b` on both accuracy and latency, and `gemma4` is newer and faster
  than `gemma3:12b` yet describes drawings too vaguely to fill `detail`. Three
  eval drawings is far too small a sample to read as an accuracy figure.
- Do not tidy `local_vision.build_prompt` without re-measuring. Two rewrites that
  read better to a human both lost accuracy: catalog-derived per-tag hints repeat
  "cup" three times for water and pushed gemma3 from 2/3 to 1/3 with everything
  tagged water, and offering an "unsure" tag made gemma4 decline all three
  drawings including one it had described correctly. A model this size takes any
  exit it is offered, and over-anchors on a repeated word.
- Never put a literal example sentence in a prompt for a small model. The schema's
  `"I would like ..., please."` came back verbatim from gemma4 and would have been
  spoken aloud; the example is now described rather than shown.
- The xAI reasoning models are unusable on the live path despite being the most
  accurate: `grok-4.6` takes 42-86s against a 15s cap. Check latency before
  swapping `XAI_MODEL`, and note `grok-4.3` is both slow and 0/3.
- Seeding `drawings_db.json` makes `score_all` parse the strokes, which an empty
  database never did, so stroke data that was previously ignored can now raise.
  `interpret` swallows that and keeps the image paths alive; do not make it fatal.
- Stroke points are `[x, y, pressure, t]` in **CSS pixels**; the PNG is `dpr`
  times larger. `t` is a shared clock across all strokes in one capture.
- Pressure is always 0.5 and `tool` is always `touch`: finger, not stylus.
- Smoothed strokes keep a `raw` array of unfiltered points. Never drop it; the
  tremor is potentially the clinically interesting signal.
- Never measure a stroke by its arc length here. Tremor adds length without
  moving the stroke anywhere, so arc length over end-to-end distance calls a
  shaky straight line bent, and a perimeter-based circularity collapses on a
  shaky circle. `stroke_geometry` fits an axis or smooths and coarsely
  resamples first, which is the only reason its numbers survive a tremor.
- Tremor smoothing needs a far smaller One Euro `beta` than the usual examples,
  because tremor is fast and a large beta raises the cutoff during the shake.
  Filter each axis with its own speed, not a shared one.
- No sound or vibration on the first stroke after a page load, by browser
  policy. The user has decided to live with it; do not "fix" it unprompted.
- iPadOS has no `navigator.vibrate`. Android is the only target.
- Speech must never be able to fail loudly. `synthesise()` returns None instead
  of raising, and a null `url` tells the tablet to speak the text itself.
- The free ElevenLabs tier requires the "Powered by ElevenLabs" line, which the
  canvas shows only when their audio actually played.
- `ELEVENLABS_BASE_URL` exists so `speech_test.py` can point synthesis at a stub.
  Nothing else should need it.
- `CaptureRecord.analysis` is filled after recognition (`text`, `tag`, `detail`,
  `confirmed`). Reloading `/viewer` shows the spoken line.
